    "problem_statement": "### Bug Report\n\n**Description**\nIt seems there's an issue with string interpolation in the Markup class. When using certain templates and data, the output is not as expected. This might be affecting how HTML tags are being escaped or processed.\n\n**Steps to Reproduce**\n\nHere's a simple script to demonstrate the problem:\n\n```python\nfrom markupsafe import Markup\n\n# Test case 1: Tuple interpolation\nresult = Markup('<em>%s:%s</em>') % ('<foo>', '<bar>')\nexpected = Markup('<em>&lt;foo&gt;:&lt;bar&gt;</em>')\nprint(\"Test 1:\", result == expected)  # Should be True\n\n# Test case 2: HTML interop\nclass Foo:\n    def __html__(self) -> str:\n        return '<em>awesome</em>'\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return 'awesome'\n\nresult = Markup('<strong>%s</strong>') % Foo()\nexpected = '<strong><em>awesome</em></strong>'\nprint(\"Test 2:\", result == expected)  # Should be True\n\n# Test case 3: String interpolation\ntemplate = '<em>%s</em>'\ndata = '<bad user>'\nexpected = '<em>&lt;bad user&gt;</em>'\nresult = Markup(template) % data\nprint(\"Test 3:\", result == expected)  # Should be True\n```\n\n**Actual Outcome**\n- The output for the above test cases is not matching the expected results, indicating a problem with how the Markup class handles interpolation and HTML escaping.\n\n**Expected Outcome**\n- The output should match the expected results, with HTML tags properly escaped and interpolated.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: 3.10.16\n- MarkupSafe version: (please specify)\n- Operating System: (please specify)\n\nThis issue might be related to recent changes in the codebase. Any insights or fixes would be appreciated!"
